# Lore, part 7: fuller entries for the last short ones.
LORE = {
'dayak': ("The Dayak are the native peoples of inland Borneo, Kenyah, Kayan, Ngaju and many more, who lived in great longhouses along the rivers and were feared as headhunters until the early twentieth century; the Ngaju kept the Kaharingan faith.",
          "They hold the Gawai harvest and the Tiwah secondary funeral, carve hornbill figures and shields, weave rattan, tattoo their bodies, and the Kenyah play the sape lute."),
'bai_naxi': ("The Bai built the Nanzhao and Dali kingdoms of Yunnan, which ruled the region between Tibet and China until the Mongols took Dali in 1253; the Naxi of Lijiang were ruled by the Mu chieftains and developed the Dongba pictographic script.",
             "The Bai serve three-course tea, bitter, sweet and aftertaste, to guests and hold the Third Month Fair in Dali; the Naxi play an orchestra of Tang and Song dynasty music and their Dongba priests still read the old pictographs."),
'afar': ("The Afar live in the Danakil depression, one of the hottest places on Earth, where the first human ancestors, like Lucy, were found; their sultanates of Aussa and Tadjoura held the trade routes to the Red Sea.",
         "They herd camels and goats in the desert, carry salt in caravans from Lake Assale to the highlands, wear the curved jile dagger, and keep a strong law of clan and hospitality."),
'torres': ("The Torres Strait Islanders are Melanesians of the islands between Australia and New Guinea; the Coming of the Light in 1871 brought Christianity, and Eddie Mabo's case overturned terra nullius in 1992.",
           "They dance with the dhari headdress that appears on their flag, sing island hymns and pearl-diving songs, hunt turtle and dugong, and feast on kup murri cooked in the ground."),
'chukchi': ("The Chukchi and Koryaks of the far north-east fought off Russian Cossacks for most of the eighteenth century and were never fully conquered by the Tsars; their homeland faces Alaska across the Bering Strait.",
            "Coastal Chukchi hunt whales and walrus from skin boats, inland Chukchi herd reindeer, and they carve walrus ivory, race reindeer sleds and hold the Kilvei festival of the reindeer."),
'pedi': ("The Pedi kingdom of the Maroteng dynasty in the Leolo mountains resisted the Boers and the British under King Sekhukhune until his defeat in 1879; their migrant workers later built the mines of the Rand.",
         "They play kiba music on reed pipes in large ensembles, keep their initiation schools, brew sorghum beer, and their women paint their houses with geometric designs."),
'purepecha': ("The Purepecha empire of Michoacan defeated the Aztecs and was never conquered by them; after the Spanish conquest the bishop Vasco de Quiroga organised its villages each around a craft.",
              "They still make copper in Santa Clara, lacquer, pottery and guitars, dance the comic Dance of the Old Men, and hold the Night of the Dead on Lake Patzcuaro with candles and boats."),
'polynesian': ("The Pacific Islanders are heirs of the Lapita voyagers and the Polynesian navigators who found every island of the Pacific, from Hawaii to Rapa Nui and New Zealand, without instruments, by stars, swells and birds.",
               "They tattoo, dance with drums and slit gongs, build outrigger canoes, cook in earth ovens, and live by the chief, the family and the church, sharing everything at great feasts."),
'ashaninka': ("The Ashaninka of the Peruvian Amazon rose with Juan Santos Atahualpa against the Spanish missions in 1742 and survived the rubber barons and the violence of the Shining Path in the 1980s and 1990s.",
              "They wear the cushma, a long cotton tunic, paint their faces with red achiote, drink masato of fermented manioc, and fight for their forest against loggers and drug traffickers."),
'moro': ("The Moro peoples of Mindanao and the Sulu archipelago, Maranao, Maguindanao, Tausug and others, had sultanates that resisted Spain for three centuries and fought the Americans after 1899.",
         "They dance the singkil between clashing bamboo poles, weave the malong, build the vinta with colourful sails, and the Maranao carve okir patterns into their houses."),
'dagomba': ("The Dagomba kingdom of northern Ghana, founded in the fifteenth century, is ruled by the Ya Na from Yendi; it became Muslim from the eighteenth century and was divided between Germany and Britain.",
            "Their drummers, the lunsi, recite the history of the kings from memory; they celebrate the Fire Festival and the Damba feast on horseback, and eat tuo zaafi with green soup."),
'tsonga': ("The Tsonga live in southern Mozambique and the Limpopo of South Africa; the Gaza empire of the Nguni ruled them in the nineteenth century, and many worked in the mines of the Rand.",
           "Their women dance xibelani with swirling layered skirts, their music, from traditional to electronic shangaan, is famous, and they cook with peanuts, cassava leaves and fish."),
'tiv': ("The Tiv are farmers of the Benue valley in Nigeria, known among anthropologists for their society without chiefs, organised by lineage; they grew to be one of Nigeria's largest peoples.",
        "They are the yam farmers of the nation, dance the swange, wear the striped black-and-white anger cloth, and tell kwagh-hir puppet stories."),
'alaskan': ("Alaska was bought by the United States from Russia in 1867; the Klondike gold rush, the Second World War and the oil of Prudhoe Bay made it, and settlers came from every state.",
            "They fish salmon and halibut, race sled dogs in the Iditarod, hunt moose, and live through winters of darkness under the northern lights and summers of midnight sun."),
'turkana': ("The Turkana and Karamojong are Nilotic herders of the dry north-west of Kenya and north-east Uganda, who resisted British control and still move with their herds across the borders.",
            "They wear bead necklaces and mud-packed headdresses with ostrich feathers, sing songs to their favourite bulls, and settle quarrels and raids by councils of elders."),
'meitei': ("The Meitei of Manipur had their own kingdom for two thousand years, whose royal chronicle the Cheitharol Kumbaba records the reigns of its kings; it adopted Vaishnavism in the eighteenth century.",
           "They dance the graceful Manipuri Ras Lila, gave the world polo, which the British learned there, hold the Lai Haraoba festival for the old gods, and fish on Loktak lake with its floating islands."),
'kashmiri': ("Kashmir was a centre of Hindu and Buddhist learning, where the chronicle Rajatarangini was written; Islam came with Sufi saints in the fourteenth century, and the valley has been disputed since 1947.",
             "They weave pashmina shawls and carpets, hold the wazwan feast of many courses, drink kahwa tea with saffron, and live on houseboats and gardens on Dal Lake."),
'luo': ("The Luo are Nilotic people who migrated from the Nile to the shores of Lake Victoria; Tom Mboya and Oginga Odinga led them in Kenya's politics, and Barack Obama's father was a Luo.",
        "They fish the lake, play benga music and the nyatiti lyre, eat fish with ugali, and their funerals last for days with music and feasting."),
'ncaucasian': ("The North Caucasians are dozens of peoples, Kabardians, Karachays, Balkars, Lezgins and more, in the mountains between the Black Sea and the Caspian, who fought the Russian conquest for decades in the nineteenth century.",
               "They dance the lezginka, keep codes of honour and hospitality, make khinkal and cheeses, and their mountain villages of stone towers are among the oldest in Europe."),
'shuar_kichwa': ("The Shuar of Ecuador resisted the Incas and the Spanish and were famous for the tsantsa, shrunken heads; the Amazonian Kichwa live along the rivers and spread the Quechua language into the forest.",
                 "They drink chicha of manioc, keep the ayahuasca ceremonies of their shamans, and have fought hard for their forest against oil and mining companies."),
'lakota': ("The Lakota and Plains nations were buffalo hunters on horseback; they defeated Custer at Little Bighorn in 1876 under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse, and suffered the massacre at Wounded Knee in 1890.",
           "They keep the Sun Dance, the sweat lodge and vision quest, the star knowledge of their ancestors, and the sacred Black Hills, which they have never given up."),
'maori': ("The Maori came to Aotearoa from eastern Polynesia around 1300; they signed the Treaty of Waitangi with Britain in 1840 and fought the New Zealand Wars over land.",
          "They perform the haka, gather on the marae with its carved meeting house, wear ta moko tattoos, cook in the hangi, and their language has revived with schools for children."),
'basque': ("The Basques kept their language, older than Indo-European, through every conquest; their fueros gave them self-government for centuries, and their sailors were great whalers and explorers.",
           "They play pelota, lift stones and chop logs in rural sports, eat pintxos and the cooking of San Sebastian, sing in choirs, and hold their identity dear."),
'assyrian': ("The Assyrians are heirs of the ancient Assyrian empire of Nineveh and among the first peoples to become Christian; they suffered the Sayfo massacres of 1915 and the wars of the twenty-first century.",
             "They speak Neo-Aramaic, close to the language of Jesus, celebrate Akitu, the spring new year, keep the Church of the East and the Chaldean church, and dance the khigga at weddings."),
'gilaki': ("The Gilaks and Mazandaranis live on the green, rainy Caspian coast of Iran, behind the Alborz mountains; their land resisted the Arab conquest longest, and the Jangal movement rose there in 1915.",
           "They grow rice and tea, eat mirza ghasemi of smoked aubergine, fish and caviar, and speak their own Iranian languages."),
'indo_trini': ("The Indo-Caribbeans descend from the Indian indentured labourers brought to Trinidad, Guyana and Suriname after slavery ended, from 1838 to 1917, mostly from Bihar and Uttar Pradesh.",
               "They celebrate Diwali with lamps and Phagwah with coloured powder, play chutney and chutney soca music, and eat roti and doubles in every street."),
'gond': ("The Gond and other Adivasi peoples of central India had their own kingdoms in Garha-Mandla, Chanda and Deogarh before the Marathas and the British; their forests were later cut for mines.",
         "They paint vivid Gond art of trees, animals and spirits, worship the forest and the clan gods, and dance the Saila and Karma."),
'lozi_tonga': ("The Lozi kingdom of Barotseland lies on the Zambezi floodplain; the Tonga of the Zambezi valley were displaced by the flooding of Lake Kariba in the 1950s.",
               "Each year the Lozi king moves to higher ground in the Kuomboka ceremony, paddled in a royal barge, and the Tonga keep the Nyami Nyami river spirit."),
'slovak': ("The Slovaks lived for a thousand years under the Kingdom of Hungary; Juraj Janosik became their Robin Hood, they joined the Czechs in 1918, and became independent in 1993.",
           "They make bryndzove halusky, dumplings with sheep cheese, play the long fujara flute, and are proud of their folk music and wooden churches."),
'ticuna_shipibo': ("The Ticuna live on the upper Amazon where Brazil, Peru and Colombia meet, and the Shipibo along the Ucayali river, where they survived missions and the rubber boom.",
                   "Shipibo women paint kene, geometric patterns that are songs made visible, and the Ticuna celebrate a girl's coming of age with the mask festival of the Pelazon."),
'chechen': ("The Chechens and Ingush fought the Russian conquest for decades, were deported to Central Asia by Stalin in 1944, and suffered two devastating wars in the 1990s and 2000s.",
            "They dance the lezginka, keep the teip clan system and nokhchalla, their code of honour, and are known for their wrestlers and fighters."),
'tatar': ("The Volga Tatars are heirs of Volga Bulgaria and the Golden Horde; the Kazan Khanate fell to Ivan the Terrible in 1552, and Tatar merchants and scholars later spread modern Islam across Russia.",
          "They celebrate Sabantuy, the plough festival, with wrestling and races, eat chak-chak and echpochmak, and the Kul Sharif mosque stands again in the Kazan Kremlin."),
'walloon': ("The Walloons are the French-speaking people of southern Belgium, heirs of the first industrial revolution on the continent, of coal and steel along the Meuse.",
            "They keep the giants processions and the Gilles of Binche at carnival, eat boulets and waffles of Liege, and brew Trappist beer."),
'yi': ("The Yi of the Liangshan mountains kept their own script, priests and a rigid caste system into the twentieth century; with the Bai they ruled the Nanzhao kingdom.",
       "They celebrate the Torch Festival with fires, bullfights and beauty contests, wear black capes, and write their ancient books in the Yi script."),
'lithuanian': ("The Grand Duchy of Lithuania was the last pagan state of Europe and one of its largest; it united with Poland, was absorbed by Russia, and was freed from the Soviets in 1990.",
               "They sing sutartines in rounds, keep Jonines at midsummer, eat cepelinai, and play basketball as their second religion."),
'ryukyuan': ("The Ryukyu Kingdom traded between China, Japan and Southeast Asia until Japan annexed it in 1879; the Battle of Okinawa in 1945 killed a quarter of the people.",
             "They play the sanshin, invented karate, eat goya and pork, and are known for long lives."),
'albanian': ("The Albanians may descend from the ancient Illyrians; Skanderbeg fought the Ottomans in the fifteenth century, and Albania became independent in 1912.",
             "They keep besa, the word of honour, sing iso-polyphony, and celebrate Flag Day on 28 November."),
'apache_oodham': ("The Apache resisted Spain, Mexico and the United States under leaders like Cochise and Geronimo; the O'odham are desert farmers of the Sonoran desert.",
                  "They hold the Apache sunrise ceremony for girls, the crown dance, and the O'odham harvest saguaro fruit for wine."),
'rakhine': ("The Rakhine had their own kingdom of Mrauk U, which traded with the Portuguese and Dutch, until the Burmese conquest of 1784.",
            "They celebrate the Thingyan water festival, are known for their Buddhist art, and the Mahamuni image was theirs."),
'latvian': ("The Latvians are a Baltic people ruled by German knights for centuries; Latvia became independent in 1918 and again in 1991 after the Baltic Way.",
            "They hold the great song and dance festival, Jani at midsummer with wreaths and caraway cheese, and eat rye bread."),
'shan': ("The Shan are a Tai people of the hills of Burma; their thirty-odd states were ruled by saopha princes until 1962.",
         "They hold the Poy Sang Long ordination, eat sticky rice and tofu, and grow tea."),
'tay_nung': ("The Tay and Nung are Tai peoples of northern Vietnam, rice farmers of the valleys, who joined the anti-French resistance.",
             "They play the dan tinh lute, sing then songs, wear indigo clothes, and live in stilt houses."),
'kpelle': ("The Kpelle are the largest people of Liberia, upland rice farmers whose Poro and Sande societies train young people in the forest.",
           "They play the triangle-frame zither and the slit drum, and tell meni-pele stories with music."),
'acehnese': ("The Sultanate of Aceh was a powerful trading state and centre of Islamic learning; it fought the Dutch for thirty years and later Indonesia, until peace in 2005.",
             "They dance the Saman, drink strong Aceh coffee, and keep sharia under special autonomy."),
'breton': ("The Bretons came from Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries; Brittany was an independent duchy until it joined France in 1532.",
           "They keep pardons, the fest-noz night dances, the bombard and biniou pipes, crepes and cider."),
'chuvash': ("The Chuvash are the only surviving speakers of the Oghur branch of Turkic, heirs of Volga Bulgaria; they became Orthodox under Russian rule.",
            "They embroider in red, wear coin headdresses, celebrate Akatuy, and brew beer for every feast."),
'greenlander': ("The New Greenlanders are a mixed people of Inuit, Danes and newcomers who settled the thawing island in the twenty-second century.",
                "They fish, mine, farm in sheltered fjords, and keep the Inuit drum dance and kaffemik gatherings alive."),
'kachin': ("The Kachin are a people of the hills of northern Burma, largely Baptist Christians, who have fought for autonomy since the 1960s.",
           "They hold the Manau festival, dancing around painted posts, and are known for jade mining."),
'occitan': ("Occitania was the land of the troubadours and of the Cathars, crushed by the Albigensian crusade in the thirteenth century.",
            "They speak the langue d'oc, cook cassoulet, play rugby, and remember the troubadours who invented courtly love."),
'ainu': ("The Ainu are the indigenous people of Hokkaido, Sakhalin and the Kurils, conquered and assimilated by Japan in the nineteenth century.",
         "They hold the iomante bear ceremony, carve wood, weave attus bark cloth, and tell yukar epics."),
'nepali': ("The Nepalis were united by Prithvi Narayan Shah of Gorkha in 1768; Nepal was never colonised, and its Gurkha soldiers became famous.",
           "They celebrate Dashain and Tihar, eat dal bhat twice a day, and fly kites in the autumn sky."),
'copt': ("The Copts are the Christians of Egypt, heirs of the ancient Egyptians; their church was founded, by tradition, by Saint Mark, and monasticism began in their deserts.",
         "They keep long fasts, the desert monasteries, and the Coptic liturgy keeps the last form of the Egyptian language."),
'timorese': ("The peoples of Timor, Flores and the Moluccas are Austronesian and Papuan; the Moluccas were the spice islands, and East Timor won independence in 2002.",
             "They weave ikat cloth, build sacred houses, and keep Catholic and ancestral traditions."),
}
